   357	No step costs a power of p. The only polylog-in-N term that is not L_p[1/2, o(1)] is
   358	λ(q) ≤ (log N)^{2+o(1)}, together with the arithmetic cost. Both sit in the explicit
   359	(log N)^{O(1)} factor, exactly as in LPP I Thm 1.1 and in Lenstra 1987.
   360	
   361	Interval fit check (step 2 of the brief). LPP needs smooth numbers in I_p, which has length
   362	p^{3/2} = x^{3/4+o(1)} around x ≈ p². Younis covers h ≥ x^θ for every fixed θ > 1/2, down to
   363	y ≥ (log x)^{K(θ)}. Our w = L_p[1/2, 1] = exp((1/√2 + o(1))√(log x log log x)) is far above
   364	(log x)^K. Both inputs fit with room to spare.
   365	
   366	## 6. Optimising the constant
   367	
   368	### 6.1 Smoothness cutoff
   369	- With w = L_p[1/2, s]: per-curve work L_p[1/2, s], success probability L_p[1/2, −1/s].
   370	- The total exponent s + 1/s is minimised at s = 1, giving 2.
   371	
   372	### 6.2 Other genera, other groups (analysis model)
   373	- **Genus g.** Orders are near p^g, in an interval of length ≍ p^{g−1/2} = x^{1−1/(2g)}.
   374	  The smoothness probability at w = L_p[1/2, s] is L_p[1/2, −g/(2s)]. The cost s + g/(2s) has
   375	  minimum √(2g), increasing in g: g = 1 gives √2, g = 2 gives 2, g = 3 gives √6 ≈ 2.449.
   376	  - For g ≥ 3 the interval exponent 1 − 1/(2g) ≥ 5/6 is inside Younis's range. So the only
   377	    missing input would be an LPP-type fibre lower bound, and even with it the constant is
   378	    worse.
   379	  - Higher genus is the wrong direction. A longer interval relative to the size does not help,
   380	    because the cost depends only on log(order)/log p = g.
   381	- **Genus 1 (ECM).** The constant would be √2, but the Hasse interval has θ = 1/2 exactly.
   382	  - Younis needs θ > 1/2, and the RH saddle-point/contour method has the same barrier: the
   383	    Perron error needs H ≥ x/h, and the horizontal and remainder terms need H ≤ x^{1/2−δ}.
   384	  - Soundararajan (RH, 2010) reaches h ≍ u√x/ρ(u/2), but only with a lower bound of z x^{−ε}
   385	    and only for y ≥ exp(5√(log x log log x)). That is not a density statement.
   386	  - So ECM stays conditional on the classical short-interval conjecture (Lenstra 1987,
   387	    Conj. (2.10)).
   388	- **Split Jacobians / E_1 × E_2.** The order is #E_1·#E_2, a product of two Hasse-interval
   389	  numbers. The smoothness probability is again L_p[1/2, −1/s], with no gain. Each factor
   390	  still needs θ = 1/2 rigour.
   391	- **Stage 2 / one large prime ≤ w².** The semismooth-to-smooth density ratio is (u log u)^{O(1)}
   392	  = L^{o(1)}, so the constant does not change.
   393	- **Order of D instead of #J.** This might be smaller, but no rigorous lower-bound handle
   394	  exists. It is an L^{o(1)} effect heuristically.
   395	- Conclusion: 2 is the floor of this route (proved for the analysis model: cost s + g/(2s)
   396	  with g ≥ 2 forced by θ > 1/2). Heuristically 2 is also the true running time of HECM, since
   397	  Younis gives an asymptotic, not only a lower bound.
   398	
   399	### 6.3 Unconditional variant (V4)
   400	LPP I Thm 1.3 (OCR of the Leiden scan; exponents partly garbled):
   401	- c₁ = (1,980,000)^{1/3} (exponent as read);
   402	- for x ≥ x₃, z = L_x[2/3, c₁] and x^{3/4} ≤ y ≤ x, there are at least
   403	  y · exp(−(log x)^{1/3}(log log x)^{−1/3}) z-smooth integers in [x, x + y].
   404	
   405	Exponent caveat: any lower bound y · exp(−o((log x)^{2/3})) suffices.
   406	- Substituting this for Lemma 5 in Prop B and §4, with w = z at x = p², gives expected time
   407	  L_p[2/3, 2^{2/3} c₁ + o(1)] (log N)^{O(1)}.
   408	- This is LPP I Thm 1.1, whose algorithmic half (part III) was never published.
   409	- Younis's unconditional Thm 1.1 (θ > 17/30, y ≥ exp(C(log x)^{2/3}(log log x)^{4/3})) gives
   410	  exp(O((log p)^{2/3}(log log p)^{4/3})). That is slightly weaker in the log log power.
   411	
   412	## 7. Literature and novelty
   413	
   414	Read directly:
   415	- **LPP I** (Phil. Trans. R. Soc. A 345 (1993) 397–408).
   416	  - Thm 1.1 claims L_p[2/3, c₀](log n)² rigorously.
   417	  - p. 398 states the conditional L_p[1/2, 2 + o(1)](log n)² under "a similar hypothesis"
   418	    on short-interval smooth numbers, and the conjectural ECM bound L_p[1/2, √2].
   419	  - So the constant 2 is LPP's.
   420	- **LPP II** (Proc. LMS 84 (2002)): Thms 1.1–1.2. The analysis is deferred to a "forthcoming
   421	  third paper".
   422	- **Pomerance, "Elliptic curves: applications and problems"** (IMA Abel Conference, Jan 2011),
   423	  slide 8: part III is "to appear (actually, 'to be written')". The method "stands (or will
