+The same proof works for H comparable to x^(3/4), so it applies to the shifted LPP interval. Set x approximately p^2 and y=L_p[1/2,s], thus c=s/sqrt(2). The density lower bound is
+
+    L_p[1/2, -k/((k-1)s)+o(1)].
+
+Using the note's algebraic propositions and choosing s=sqrt(k/(k-1))>1 gives expected factoring time
+
+    L_p[1/2, 2 sqrt(k/(k-1))+o(1)] (log N)^O(1).
+
+One explicit algorithm with leading constant 2 is obtained by dovetailing these algorithms for k=2,3,... with time shares 2^(-k). For each fixed k the scheduling loss is constant; taking k arbitrarily large proves a 2+o(1) leading constant. This argument does not supply the note's explicit O((log log p)^(-1/2)) rate.

