codex
1. **OK — VALID: LPP II quantifiers and Lemma 4/Proposition B.**  
   §2’s “all but at most p integers z” correctly transcribes Theorem 1.1: prime \(p>8100\), at most \(p\) exceptions in the stated interval, and at least
   \[
   \frac{p^{9/2}}{24000(\log p)^2(\log\log p)^2}
   \]
   quintics for every remaining integer. Quintic means degree exactly five, with arbitrary nonzero leading coefficient; \(J_f\) is the Jacobian of the smooth projective model of \(y^2=f(x)\). Lemma 4’s removal of multiples of \(\ell\) and exceptions leaves the claimed \(q^{3/2}/3\) values. Its constant \(144000\), and Proposition B, check out. [LPP II, pp. 105–107](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper129.pdf).

2. **OK — VALID in the required range: Younis and Lemma 5.**  
   §2’s first displayed estimate and its quantifiers match Theorem 1.3: fixed \(1/2<\theta\le1\), some \(K(\theta)>2\), uniformly \(x^\theta\le h\le x\), \((\log x)^K\le y\le2x\). The omitted second estimate concerns the \(h\)-independent function \(f_\theta\).

   “We take θ = 3/4” is legitimate: \(x<p^2\), hence \(h=p^{3/2}>x^{3/4}\). Here \(y=L_x[1/2,1/\sqrt2+o(1)]\), above Hildebrand’s threshold and below \(x^{1/2-\varepsilon}\). Also \(\alpha=1-o(1)\). In §7.1, \(H=x^{1+2/(\varepsilon K)}/h\) leaves ample room for the remainder bounds. Hildebrand enters through the **long** interval \(h=x\); there is no circular reliance on the desired short interval. The large-\(y\) split is irrelevant here. I found no defect in this portion of the proof; some adaptations of earlier lemmas are abbreviated. [Younis, Theorem 1.3 and §7](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.05761v1).

3. **MINOR — GAP in Lemma 3’s wording; separation is VALID.**  
   “Ends without a NonUnit” should require that the run **passes the leading-coefficient and discriminant checks and enters step 5**. Otherwise a discriminant gcd equal to \(N\) can terminate without a NonUnit, without defining smooth reductions.

   After this correction, reduction commutes with the checked arithmetic even for nonsquarefree \(N\). Nilpotents cause no escape: a nonzero nonunit leading coefficient yields a proper gcd. Lemma 1’s affine-point weighting and CRT independence are correct.

   Proposition A’s condition “ℓ ∤ #J_f(F_q)” suffices. An affine \(P\) has \([P-\infty]\ne0\); the increasing prime schedule visits both \(k_0\) and \(k_0\ell\). Double-and-add does not remove these outer checkpoints. Their incompatible identity reductions force a detected coefficient. An additional check on **75 covered nonsquarefree cases** found no violation.

4. **MINOR — GAP in runtime presentation, not the leading constant.**  
   §4’s “Growth of the cost” should compare an **explicit deterministic cost envelope**, not take ratios of quantities specified only by \(O(\cdot)\) and \(o(1)\). That envelope has adjacent ratios tending to one, so the geometric expected-time sum works.

   The bookkeeping otherwise checks: \(\delta(p)=O(\log\log\log p/\log\log p)=o(\varepsilon(p))\), and
   \[
   \lambda(q)\le(\log N)^2(\log\log N)^2
   \]
   even when \(q\) is nearly \(N\). Thus the \((\log N)^3\) repetition budget suffices. Handle the finitely many primes below the asymptotic threshold separately, once \(w_j\) exceeds their Weil bounds. No power-of-\(p\) loss appears; the stated polylogarithmic accounting is consistent.

5. **OK — VALID: R1 and R2.**  
   R1’s \(2\sqrt\beta\) conversion is correct for fixed \(\beta\); bounded and slowly growing \(p\) do not destroy uniformity. Dovetailing with the unconditional Lenstra–Pomerance algorithm gives the minimum of the two expected bounds. [Lenstra–Pomerance 1992](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf).

   R2’s “second-largest distinct prime factor” is the right parameter: every divisor with two distinct prime factors has its least factor at most \(P_2(N)\). Polynomially many splits, primality tests and perfect-power reductions are absorbed. For prime-power inputs, state the polynomial-time case separately because \(P_2(N)\) is undefined.

6. **OK within the stated model; GAP if interpreted universally: §6 optimisation.**  
   “2 is the floor of this route” is justified for the model
   \[
   s+\frac{g}{2s},\qquad \min=\sqrt{2g}.
   \]
   It is not a lower bound for every conceivable factoring method.

   The suggested alternatives provide no demonstrated improvement: an elliptic curve over \(\mathbf F_{p^2}\) has order \(p^2+O(p)\), retaining interval exponent \(1/2\); Weil restriction preserves that order. General abelian surfaces still have order about \(p^2\). An accessible elliptic subvariety returns to the Hasse-interval problem. A family forcing a sufficiently large, known smooth subgroup could alter the calculation, but an efficient construction and suitable distribution theorem would be additional substantial inputs. I found no rigorous RH-only constant below \(2\) through these alternatives.

7. **MAJOR — ERROR in the claimed analytic novelty.**  
   §7’s “Younis’s RH theorem appears to be the first such input” overlooks what follows from **Soundararajan’s weighted proof plus classical divisor moments**.

   Even with his printed cutoff \(y\ge e^{5\sqrt{\log x\log\log x}}\), Cauchy–Schwarz and Shiu give an \(L[1/2]\)-scale density lower bound in intervals of length \(x^{3/4}\), yielding an older RH-conditional factoring bound with, for example, constant \(5\sqrt2+\sqrt2/5\approx7.354\). The quoted \(hx^{-\varepsilon}\) is not the strongest consequence of that proof. [Soundararajan 2010](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1009.1591), [divisor-moment bound, equation (3.1)](https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2021/16/12330/5558145).

   More strongly, I checked a reworking of his support to
   \[
   \sqrt{x}y^{-a}\le m\le\sqrt{x}y^{-b},\qquad b<a<1/2.
   \]
   For \(y=L_x[1/2,c]\), the necessary inequality holds when \(bc^2>1/4\). Hölder with fixed moment \(k\) then gives factoring constant \(2\sqrt{k/(k-1)}\). Dovetailing over \(k\) yields \(2+o(1)\). The derivation is in [the scratch proof](./soundararajan_support.md). **This is my deduction from older ingredients, not a located prior published formulation.**

   I found no explicit earlier statement of Theorem R, no Younis-to-HECM application, and no published LPP III. The [2011 talk](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/tatetalk1.pdf) supports “unwritten then,” not “never written.” I also found no unconditional almost-all-\(p\) genus-2 theorem at this smoothness scale; general almost-all interval results do not automatically cover prime-square centres.

8. **MINOR — ERROR in §6.3’s transcription; V4 survives.**  
   The quoted density loss with “\((\log\log x)^{-1/3}\)” is wrong. LPP I Theorem 1.3 actually states
   \[
   c_1=1980000^{1/3},\quad z=L_x[2/3,c_1],\quad x^{3/5}\le h\le x,
   \]
   with at least
   \[
   h\exp[-(\log x)^{1/3}(\log\log x)^{8/3}]
   \]
   smooth integers. Both misread exponents should be corrected. The loss remains lower order than the \(L[2/3]\) exponent, so V4 and its leading constant \(2^{2/3}c_1\) remain valid. [LPP I, p. 399](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/hyperI.pdf).

**Overall verdict:** **(a)** I found no fatal error in Theorem R; its proof needs small formal repairs, and the required part of Younis’s RH argument appears sound. **(b)** The novelty discussion needs substantial revision. The mechanism and constant were already LPP’s, and an RH \(2+o(1)\) bound can be recovered from older ingredients by the deduction above. A prior explicit published formulation remains unlocated; Younis supplies a cleaner analytic input and the note’s quantitative error bookkeeping.
