+# Referee deduction from pre-2024 ingredients
+
+This is a deduction checked in this audit, **not** a claim that a prior paper states the resulting factoring theorem. Sources: Soundararajan, arXiv:1009.1591 (2010), especially the estimate following (5), and Shiu's fixed divisor-moment bound (1980; also stated as (3.1) in Matomäki–Shao, IMRN 2021, doi:10.1093/imrn/rnz188).
+
+Put T = sqrt(log x log log x), y = exp(c T), u = log x/log y, and H = x^(3/4). Fix 0 < b < a < 1/2, and replace Soundararajan's Dirichlet polynomial by
+
+    M(s) = sum_{sqrt(x) y^(-a) <= m <= sqrt(x) y^(-b), P+(m)<=y} m^(-s).
+
+Write R = rho(u/2) and L = sqrt(x) y^(-b). Hildebrand's long-interval smooth-number asymptotic and partial summation give
+
+    M(1) >>_{a,b} R log y.
+
+Indeed log m/log y lies between u/2-a and u/2-b, so the Dickman density is at least R; the logarithmic width is (a-b) log y. This is safely inside the known unconditional range of the long-interval asymptotic.
+
+Define delta by x exp(2 delta) = x+H. Soundararajan's positive integral I counts triples n=r m1 m2 with weight Lambda(r) times a triangular weight at most delta. Such r satisfy r <= (1+H/x)y^(2a) <= y for large x, so every counted n is y-smooth. Hence, with W = sum_{x<n<=x+H, P+(n)<=y} tau_3(n),
+
+    I <= delta (log y) W.
+
+Under RH the proof leading to (5), with the new upper support L, gives
+
+    I >= x delta^2 M(1)^2
+         - O(sqrt(x) delta^2 (log x)(L+1/delta) M(1)).
+
+The mean-value estimates use only the support upper bound L. In the auxiliary integral J, the term J2 remains nonpositive: its Dirichlet-series coefficients have sign minus Lambda(n), and its Mellin kernel is nonnegative. Thus excluding off-diagonal terms, as in the original argument for very small delta, is unnecessary here. The residue in J1 is bounded using M(0)<=L. Dyadic summation gives the displayed bound for all zeros; the integral on Re(s)=-1/2 is smaller.
+
+The two relative errors tend to zero provided
+
+    y^b R / u -> infinity,
+    H R / (u sqrt(x)) -> infinity.
+
+The second condition holds since H=x^(3/4). Since
+
+    log R = -(1/(4c)+o(1)) T,
+
+the first holds when b c^2 > 1/4. Such b<a<1/2 exist for every fixed c>1/sqrt(2). Consequently W >> H R^2 log y.
+
+For any fixed integer k>=2, Shiu gives
+
+    sum_{x<n<=x+H} tau_3(n)^k <<_k H (log x)^(3^k-1).
+
+Hölder therefore implies
+
+    Psi(x+H,y)-Psi(x,y)
+      >>_{a,b,k} H R^(2k/(k-1)) (log x)^(-O_k(1)).
+
+The same proof works for H comparable to x^(3/4), so it applies to the shifted LPP interval. Set x approximately p^2 and y=L_p[1/2,s], thus c=s/sqrt(2). The density lower bound is
+
+    L_p[1/2, -k/((k-1)s)+o(1)].
+
+Using the note's algebraic propositions and choosing s=sqrt(k/(k-1))>1 gives expected factoring time
+
+    L_p[1/2, 2 sqrt(k/(k-1))+o(1)] (log N)^O(1).
+
+One explicit algorithm with leading constant 2 is obtained by dovetailing these algorithms for k=2,3,... with time shares 2^(-k). For each fixed k the scheduling loss is constant; taking k arbitrarily large proves a 2+o(1) leading constant. This argument does not supply the note's explicit O((log log p)^(-1/2)) rate.

